//! The WebView2 backend (Windows): each view is an Edge WebView2 controller in a native child
//! window of the platform-window window it opens in, drawn over the Silk UI at its hosted node
//! and clipped to the node's visible part. The C side is native/windows/bridge.c; the Silk side is
//! `bridge.BridgeWebViews`, shared with the WebKit backend.
//!
//! Start platform-window's platform (`BridgePlatform`, Win32) and open the window first; views
//! live in its windows and run on its thread. WebView2 starts each view asynchronously: `open`
//! returns at once, `Created` follows when the engine is ready, and commands sent before that run
//! then, in order. Every event the bridge queues wakes the window's `nextEvent` (which may then
//! return `None`), so drain `takeEvents` after each turn of the loop.
//!
//! Agent commands go through the Chrome DevTools Protocol: the agent script runs in an isolated
//! world of its own, which pages cannot see, and clicks, keys and wheel turns are the protocol's
//! input events, which pages get as trusted input.
//!
//! The program needs the Edge WebView2 Runtime (part of Windows 11) and `WebView2Loader.dll`
//! beside it; without either, `open` fails with `Unsupported`.
import bridge { BridgeWebViews }
pub import bridge { ViewFrame }

pub type WindowsWebViews = BridgeWebViews
